In <199309230900.AA17840@panix.com>, ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu (Eli Brandt)  wrote:

> I don't understand the logical step being made here.  If people
> were observed to solve the TSP in polynomial time, this hypothesis
> would make sense.  If people instead take exponential time to solve
> exponential-time problems, does that not suggest instead that
> "quantum thought" is not going on?  At the least, this would not
> seem to be an argument *for* it.

1.  A quantum computer cannot solve the TSP problem in   
    reasonable time, or any NP complete problem.  I never said
    that it could.  It can solve some NP problems in
    polynomial time.

2.  The perception problem (non linear inverse problem) is not
    NP complete, but it is NP by all known algorithms, yet
    quite simple animals can effortlessly solve this problem in
    polynomial time.

Finding the global minimum of a large dimensional function
in the case where the global minimum is substantially
deeper than any local minimum is equivalent to solving the
non linear inverse problem.

On the other hand, it is difficult to explain how the brain
could function as a quantum computer, because its
components are much too heavy and slow, and the
temperatures too high.  A more conservative explanation is
that some magic algorithm will turn up that will enable
us to solve this class of problem.

However I notice that people who go looking for algorithms
for this class of problem tend to give up on AI pretty
fast.

This of course does not prove that no such algorithm
exists, but people have been looking for such an algorithm
for a long time, and I do not expect it to be found anytime
soon.  On the other hand people were looking for a proof of
Fermat's last theorem for centuries, and in the end they
found it.

