From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: "Experiment of interest to AI and quantum computer designers"

> 2.  The perception problem (non linear inverse problem) is not
>     NP complete, but it is NP by all known algorithms, yet
>     quite simple animals can effortlessly solve this problem in
>     polynomial time.

It is meaningless to call an technique "polynomial time" unless you
can vary the size of the problem it's solving -- increasing it
without bound, strictly speaking.  The static vision problem, in
contrast, involves input from a fixed number of photoreceptors.
Roughly and informally, a constant-size problem is solved in
constant time.  (One could, conversely, define problem size in terms
of scene complexity.  Larger problems now result in longer solution
times; it is not clear to me that a quantification of the values
would show polynomial performance.)  Doubling the number of rods and
cones in the retina would not cause any difference to the time
performance of visual perception.  These extra inputs would simply
have nowhere to go.  You'd need to increase the capacity of the next
layer of the visual system, and hence the next, and...

This leads into a simple, even obvious, explanation of how brains do
complicated things in short times: they're highly parallel.
Specifically, the parallelism in the perceptual system is such that
the "problem size" provided by sensory input can be dealt with in a
reasonable amount of time.  Notice that creatures with better visual
capabilities frequently have larger visual, uh, cortices.

