In regards to whether we should or should not trade with China:

Recent history suggests that a totalitarian regime such as the Soviet
Union has a mean time to failure of two generations.  In my opinion, the
issue is whether China would fall faster by trading with it than by not
trading with it.  Again, in my opinion, what can precipitate the fall
of a totalitarian regime is either too little economic progress
(as in the Soviet case), or too much.  At a sufficiently high standard
of living, people have the means to subvert a totalitarian regime -
desktop publishing, faxes, telephones, etc.  They also will have a higher
level of education needed to run a more advanced economy - and I believe
it is no co-incidence that universities are often the site of revolts.

I believe that the capitalist invasion of China has already begun, 
starting with beach-heads such as Hong Kong and Taiwan offloading work
to the parts of China nearest them.  Sensitive indicators of economic
growth, such as the demand for commerical airplanes (which depends on
the number of people flying, which rises faster than GNP), show China
is growing rapidly economically.  If it maintains an 8% growth rate
I give it a generation until the country has a high enough standard of
living to overthrow the present political system.  As a supporting
example, I point to northern Mexico.  That part of Mexico has advanced
economically realtive to the rest of the country because of industry
offloaded from the US.  It is that part of Mexico that is also the
most prone to vote against the PRI - the political party that controlled
Mexico for generations.

Since I think the economic growth route to changing China's government
is faster (one generation rather than two), I support open trade and
investment with them.  Unfortunately for non-thinking liberals, either
way it will take longer than they would like - most seem to think
economic sanctions will have some sort of immediate effect.

Dani Eder

